  AND THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT MAKES THE PING MEANINGFUL.  The ping fails in BOTH states, so "the
  ping failed" proves nothing by itself -- which is exactly why the queue forbade resting on it.
  What changed is WHO reports the failure:
    feature OFF (step 1b): "From 23.1.1.2" -- swi_b itself, unable to resolve.  The packets
                           never left swi_b.
    feature ON  (step 3):  "From 23.1.1.1" -- the DUT.  swi_b resolved 23.1.1.3 to the DUT's
                           MAC and FORWARDED the echo requests to it; the DUT accepted them,
                           tried to resolve the real 23.1.1.3 on vlan23 (the 3 broadcast
                           requests from 23.1.1.1 in the capture), found nothing, and returned
                           the unreachable itself.
  So the identical ping output text carries opposite meanings, and only the source address and
  the capture separate them.
  STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS.  The authored expected result is met in full.

STEP 4 -- THE MASK BOUNDARY.  The case's /24-vs-/16 mismatch makes BackupSW treat all of
          23.0.0.0/16 as on-link.  Does the DUT local-proxy-answer for an address inside
          BackupSW's /16 but OUTSIDE its own /24?

  tool: sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L2-mask-boundary --out /tmp/ck1747/T43849 \
          --iface eth3 --arp 23.2.2.2,23.1.1.3 --sender-ip 23.1.1.5 \
          --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 --count 3 --wait 5
  cap L2-mask-boundary-eth3.pcap: 9 frame(s)
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 23.2.2.2 tell 23.1.1.5   [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 23.1.1.3 tell 23.1.1.5   [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REPLY 23.1.1.3 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
  Corroborated from swi_b: `ping 23.2.2.2` left swi_b's ARP table with no entry for it.

  Reading: 23.2.2.2 -> 0 replies; 23.1.1.3 -> 3 replies, in the same leg.  `ip local-proxy-arp`
  is bounded by the DUT INTERFACE's own subnet (23.1.1.0/24), not by the neighbour's idea of
  on-link.  So the deliberate mask mismatch in the case text works only for addresses inside the
  DUT's /24; a BackupSW host that ARPs for 23.2.x.x gets nothing and is simply unreachable.
  Worth knowing before anyone designs a one-armed-router deployment on a mismatched mask.
  STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS (boundary measured, behaviour is correct and gated).

