VERDICT: PASS -- the authored expected result of AWP-4357 is met in full on real hardware: with
`ip local-proxy-arp` on the DUT's vlan23 (23.1.1.1/24) and BackupSW at 23.1.1.2/16 over VLAN 23,
`ping 23.1.1.3` from BackupSW fails and BackupSW's ARP table holds 23.1.1.3 -> e01a.ead4.7043,
the DUT's MAC.  Beneath that, the ARP exchange itself was measured on the wire: the identical
crafted request drew 0 replies with the feature off and 3 replies carrying the DUT's own MAC
with it on, while a no-route control drew none in either state.  Because the ping fails in BOTH
states, the ping alone proves nothing -- what separates them is the source of the unreachable,
23.1.1.2 (BackupSW itself, feature off) versus 23.1.1.1 (the DUT, feature on), corroborated by
the DUT's own broadcast ARP requests for 23.1.1.3 in the capture.  The feature is bounded by the
DUT interface's own subnet: an address inside BackupSW's /16 but outside the DUT's /24 is never
answered.  It is measurably distinct from plain `ip proxy-arp`, which refused the same-subnet
target in T43848 on this same DUT.  Removal is proven in running-config, on the wire and in
BackupSW's table, and the whole matrix reproduces after a reboot.  Both devices are restored and
diffed identical, and the spanning tree is back to its pre-case shape.
